    "insert": "\nDevelop a deep understanding of the current state of AI integration in healthcare workflows, including its strengths, limitations and research gaps. Clearly identify and explain SOTA weaknesses highlight the unique contribution of the proposed resarch and present a coherent combination of existing materials to emphasize added value.\nThe SOTA presented, cites relevant studies to demonstrate applications of AI in healthcare (e.g. summarizing patient histories and generating diagnostic pathways). Through the effective use of cites, you underscore AI's potential and limitations, such as ethical concerns, interpretability issues and lack of real world validation. The exposé must maintain relevance to the research questions by integrating SOTA's insights into the proposed framework. The SOTA identifies critical gaps (e.g. limited frameworks and verification challenges) and aligns with the exposé's focus on improving collaboration between AI and clinicians. Insufficient real world validation is mentioned in the SOTA but needs a connection to the proposed framework, how you want to solve these research gaps.\nAdd explicit connections between identified SOTA weaknesses and your unique contributions. For example, specify how your framework will address challenges like interpretability and practical validation. Reflect on the adequacy of SOTA by reviewing additional recent studies to support the exposés claims.\n\nConstruct a logically structured theoretical framework that aligns with the research questions and demonstrates the relevance of selected theories to collaborative AI in healthcare.\nTheoretical approaches (e.g. SHAP, LIME) are presented to address interpretability and trust in AI and bioethical principles guide framework designs. The theoretical framework addresses directly the research focus on collaborative AI, supporting interpretability and ethical considerations in healthcare settings. Ensure the framework brings technical capabilities into harmony with practical challenges. The ethical and technical aspects are justified, though specific use cases like radiology need's more emphasizing. There is limited elaboration how the chosen theoretical models will resolve research gaps in SOTA.\nExpand the theoretical framework to adress radiology and treatment planning. Reflect on wether the the theoretical framework can address edge cases and challenges identified in the methodology.\n\nDevelop a methodology that effectively addresses the research questions and ensures applicability in clinical settings. Provide clear, detailed explanations of chosen methods, address potential challenges and propose solutions to ensure practical feasibility.\nYour methodology uses quantitative approaches (like accuracy comparisons and workflow impact) and qualitative approaches (like expert interviews). Framework development and evaluation through simulations are outlined as equally. The approach aligns with the research goals focusing on improving efficiency and trust in AI assisted systems. A clear understanding of data privacy regulations and system interpretability has been shown. Existing materials are appropriately referenced to justify methodology choices, though clear validation values would support the approach. The potential difficulties section identifies general challenges for edge cases and data protection but lacks implementation in clinical contexts.\nEnhance the methodology by demonstrate how quantitative and qualitative methods will complement each other for robust results. Include details how the edge cases will be addressed such as introducing testing cycles for example. Regularly assess the methodology's effectiveness during research phases.\n\n"
